losest performance  to the desired performance specified by  the user  each device has eight different  parameters so we're going to tweak those  parameters a little bit each  by increasing and decreasing them by  normalized and equal steps creating 16  new designs  then we use our neural networks to  predict the performance of  all of these newly generated designs and  the one that got us closest  to the desired performance will be uh  sort of taken as a starting point  for our second iteration so we'll  continue this process  until our neural networks are able to  sort of find a design  that accurately uh sort of  delivers the desired performance so how  well  uh does it work  um so at the first step to sort of test  this  we use our software to specify uh just  drop a diameter  uh ranging from droplet sizes of  25 microns all the way up to 200 microns  and then we fabricated and tested the  device that was suggested by the  software  and as you can see on on the figure  where we're keeping a really good trend  of of delivering the drop of diameter  that was specified by the user in  addition  you can also specify droplet diameter  and generation rate at the same time  so for these graphs we are specifying a  droplet diameter of 100 microns  and a generation rate ranging from  50 droplets per second all the way up to  uh  500 droplets per second and  well we can do the same thing for a drop  of diameter of 75 microns the same  generation rates and dropped the  diameter of 50 microns  with the same generation rates and sort  of to summarize these  in numbers on average we observed an  experimental error  of around 5 for uh for the  for the droplet diameter and around ten  percent for the generation rate so  if user specifies the droplet diameter  and generation rate  we're able to deliver that performance  uh  uh with an average error of five percent  and uh ten percent for drop diameter and  uh generation rate uh respectively  so uh we made this also into a software  where you can go to our website and  specify your  desired diameter and generation rate and  hit generation  hit generate design and you can see you  get your  geometry you can also open that design  in a software created  in our lab called three dev and then  download that design  in a format of svg files that you can  directly load to the software that  controls the cnc mill  now you have to fill around with the  settings a little bit just to make sure  your g-codes  are placed correctly but then you can  rapidly prototype that  microflip device and assemble it  as you can see here and then that device  would deliver that desired performance  so here we wanted 50 hertz and 150  microns 150 hertz  and as you can see we're getting really  close to that you can also have some  design constraints for example for  single cell encapsulation we want our  device to be as  shallow as possible uh so here we're  doing an aspect ratio of one which is  the minimum in our software  to keep the cells in the uh and the sort  of the plane of focus you can see an  entirely new design is suggested for the  same performance  it can also give you the cell  concentration you need to run your  experiment at  and you can see we're getting single  cell encapsulation and also we're  getting droplet diameters of  46.3 microns and 167 hertz  uh which is a basically really close to  our desired performance so  we can have uh design automation with or  without constraints  it is an online tune and also uh and  also uh  we can sort of calculate the the cell  concentrations you need to  run your experiments at to achieve a  single cell encapsulation  so when you use our software and specify  your desired  performance a design will be suggested  to you  and uh the the sort of the performance  you're going to get out of that device  is going to be ultimately  dependent on the tolerances in your  fabrication and  also testing meaning how accurate your  pumps are  and using our neural networks we're  actually able to  predict how these tolerances would  affect your  observed performance for example here  we're assuming your tolerance  your fabrication has about 10 percent  error in  uh and sort of spatial accuracy so so we  can predict  how having an oil and lip width 10  larger or smaller  would affect your uh observed droplet  diameter  we can also do this uh sort of very  efficiently for  uh all of the other design parameters  for both droplet diameter and generation  rate  and creating these sort of like  tolerance plots  that you can use to understand how  tolerances and fabrication  will affect your observed performance we  can also use these  information provided here to identify  what are the main parameters you have to  care about or make sure you're doing  accurately  to achieve that to achieve that desired  performance for example in in the case  that we are showing here  for droplet diameter your oil inlet  width is the most dominant parameter  so once you fabricate your device you  just want to make sure to look at the  device under the microscope make sure  your inlet width  is what we are suggesting  and then you should be good to go uh  another  observation that can be made here is um  these dominant parameters will change  based on  what is your design and what is your  desired performance so it's not going to  be  always take a look at the oil in a  little bit sometimes going to be the  orifice or  other parameters and that goes to show  like uh  the the the sort of the the level of uh  sophistication a design automation tool  like this can bring  that gives you performance specific  information on your device and how you  should fabricate it  and then ultimately we can also use our  neural networks to predict how  changing the flow rates uh increasing  both the fluid florida oil and water  would affect your droplet diameter and  generation rate so  in case if you have tolerances in your  fabrication  you can use these plots as for  as guidelines to bring different the  observed performance  back to your desired performance so  all of this is great but well how  generalizable  is it so traditionally  or with traditional machine learning  let's say we we required 500 data points  to develop an  accurate neural network that can predict  the performance of droplet generation  and if you want to change your oil or if  you want to change  uh your fluid from one type to another  one would expect you would need another  500 data points  to sort of achieve the same level of  performance accuracy  however with uh  with transfer learning um  since the high level dynamics of droplet  generation would stay the same  regardless of the types of fluids you  can use we can do the generalizability  from one fluid to another  much more efficiently and if we have  a model already trained on  droplet generation then we can  generalize that  uh with significantly fewer number of  data points to  uh to to new fluids and you can think  about it  as this way it would be much easier to  understand or to learn how to  ride a motorcycle if you already know  how to ride a bike  and the way transfer learning itself  works  it's actually uh pretty simple so assume  a typical  feed forward neural network  and what's generally agreed upon is  the first few hidden layers carry the  more generic  information to your system so this is  going to be  if your neural network is like a  classifier for  like cats versus dog or is it a model  for predicting  the performance of droplet generation  and the last few hidden layers  uh carry more specific information so  this could be about  your fluid properties or the the sort of  the material properties used for  your microfluidic devices to create  these droplets so  in transfer learning what we do is we  don't allow the first few hidden layers  to be updated when we are training our  model so  we just keep the keep those layers as  frozen layers  and just allow the last couple of hidden  layers to be updated to our new system  and because we don't really require to  train for so many  additional weights now we only need a  few weights to be updated  on our neural network this requires uh  much  much fewer uh data points so to see if  actually transfer learning uh works in  droplet microflicks what we did  is uh if you remember we had a large  scale data set  on on generating droplets using gi water  and  nf 350 mineral oil so to test the  applicability of transfer learning and  droplet generation we created  two new small scale data sets the first  one  we swapped out dli water for lv  bacterial media  and produced droplets with lb bacterial  media  creating about 36 data points and then  in our second small scale data set  we change the oil from nf-350 mineral  oil  to light mineral oil and the viscosity  difference between these two oils  is actually a factor of two so these  oils are  significantly different uh in their  fluid property  and as you can see when training neural  networks from scratch  our models are not really doing a good  job on on accurately predicting the  performance for these small scale data  sets and that's sort of given  because nobody expecting uh neural  networks to  work uh good with uh only having like 40  or 20 data points  however when we use transfer learning  and update our pre-trained modeled with  this new small scale data sets  we can see our models are doing a much  much better job in comparison to  training from scratch when using  uh transfer landing so we have done  sort of the heavy lifting on  understanding or capturing the high  level dynamics of droplet generation  with a large-scale data set  and now uh us or or different users can  create these small-scale data sets of  let's say  40 50 or something around that data  points  to uh sort of generalize this and update  our models to  to sort of uh be able to predict the  performance of droplet generators  for uh different types of fluids  so everything we talked about today was  about a single component in a single  type of  uh of in a single type of microwave so  we're doing  uh design automation for specifically  flow focusing droplet generators  and uh there are so many different  components in just throughout the  microwave  itself and also there are so many  different kinds of microwaves like  inertial microfluidics acoustic  microfluidics  or capillary microfluidics so  this cannot be done by a single person  single lab or even a single institution  and it really requires a sort of a  community  of people sort of to start working  together to share data and also  create open source uh tools like uh like  dfd  um that can uh sort of uh really  accelerate the  uh the the sort of the design process in  microfluidics and then enable  uh sort of downstream processes like  synthetic biology that we talked about  earlier that could really benefit  from not having to spend too much time  on developing  microfluidic devices so towards this end  we made sure all of our software is open  source  and also all of the data we created can  be  freely accessed and downloaded on our  website so  make sure to check out our website our  lab website ciderlab.org  our tool website daptycad.org and if  you're interested to see  sort of our source code you can go to  our github  as well so uh with that i would like to  uh  thank my pi dr doug densmore my great  colleagues at ciderlab and damplab  and also uh man my funding  a set of agencies and with that i would  like to uh thank you all for your time  and attention
 

